WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.202
-Welcome to the show
and thanks for talking with me.

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:04.670
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.972
-For those who don't know, you
are a songwriter, a performer,

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.840
a producer as well.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.345
And you've worked with
Selena Gomez, Camila Cabello,

00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:16.649
Halsey, and most famously,
I think, is probably

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:19.285
your number-one collaborator
is Billie Eilish, your sister.

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:22.122
-That's right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:23.756
-When did you guys first realize

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:26.493
that you would be good working
together, you and Billie?

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:29.829
-Well, I always knew she had
an incredibly beautiful voice.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:32.431
But I didn't want to force her
into child labor.

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:36.536
So I kind of waited
until she wanted to. You know?

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.537
-How much older are you
than Billie?

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:39.638
-Four years.
-Four years.

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:41.073
And you saw it
when you were a kid.

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.708
You were like,
"Oh, she's got something."

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.543
-Her voice was always so much
better than mine.

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:46.846
And it's just like one of those
where you're like, "Oh, yeah.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.047
That's just the way it is, huh?"

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.315
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.451
And then when she seemed
interested in recording,

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:54.553
I was like, "Well, let's try
some stuff out."

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:55.587
-And what did you do?

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:56.889
You just put something --

00:00:56.889 --> 00:00:59.192
Like, where did you perform
and what did you put out?

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.027
-Well, the first couple things
we did

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:01.994
just lived on
the website SoundCloud.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:03.463
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:07.099
-We did one song that hopefully
people don't know very well

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.968
because we're not
super pumped on it.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.937
And then we did another one
that, hopefully,

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:12.505
people also don't know
very well.

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.074
-You don't have to
say the names.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:16.008
-And the third one we did
was "Ocean Eyes,"

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:19.545
which people heard.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:20.846
-And how many people --

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:22.715
When did you realize,
"Oh, we got something here"?

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.884
-Well, we put it out
because we had no schedule.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.519
We weren't on a label
or anything.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.721
We just put it out one night
and then went to bed

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:30.323
and then woke up
the next morning

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:32.392
and it had like 1,000 plays
and we were like,

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:36.462
"I don't know 1,000 people.
Do you know 1,000 people?"

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.130
But it was really --

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.265
Looking back, it was so gradual
in terms of that.

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:41.433
But that was what made it
so fun.

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:42.768
You know what I mean?

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:46.172
I always feel kind of bad
for someone who wakes up with

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.508
like 10 million views on
their yodeling in a Walmart.

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:49.676
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:51.211
-Yeah, yeah, I understand
what you're saying.

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:52.879
-It just seems like
it goes so fast

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.980
that you don't get time
to appreciate little --

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.115
Like, a thousand's a ton.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.883
If you had 1,000 people
in your house,

00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.252
you'd be like, "Please, leave."

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.853
[ Laughter ]
-This is true.

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:02.521
But look at what
you end up doing.

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:05.224
Your debut album, "When We Fall
Asleep, Where Do We Go?"

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:06.793
debuted at number one,

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.562
has been streamed
over 15 billion times worldwide.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.230
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.065
15 billion times.

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:16.903
But the way you put a song
together is very interesting.

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.304
I was asking you about this.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:20.806
You told me that you would
record different sounds

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:22.942
and Billie would record
different sounds.

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.877
And you'd go, "Hey, can we use
this in the song?"

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:26.111
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:26.111 --> 00:02:29.515
Well, I mean, because computers
and everything

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.984
have gotten so much cheaper
and more accessible,

00:02:31.984 --> 00:02:34.920
I feel like a lot of music
producers have the same toolbox.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.056
And I think, like,
to me, as a producer,

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.425
I always want something
to set my stuff apart.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.161
And so I'll walk around with
just an iPhone will work,

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:44.630
but sometimes I'll bring
a little mobile recorder.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.333
And if I hear an interesting
sound, I'll just record it,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.102
and then later I'll listen
through them and I'll go like,

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:51.570
"I wonder how I can use that."

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.440
So, like, there's a song of
Billie's called "Bury a Friend."

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.176
And we both have Invisalign,
dental teeth straighteners.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:02:59.979
And she was at the dentist,
and they were grinding down --

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.048
They put these attachments
on your teeth,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:05.084
and they were grinding down of
hers with that horrible, like --

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.520
Like, whatever ASMR is,
it's the opposite of that.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.388
The most unpleasant --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:10.122
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:11.424
-Like...
[ Mimics drill ]

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.593
Like, that sound.
-Yeah. It's in your head.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.861
-She came home from
one of those appointments

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:18.531
and was like, "I recorded it,"
and I was like, "Great."

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.532
And we put it right in
"Bury a Friend."

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:21.734
-Let me show you guys.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.036
So, here's the sound
that Billie recorded.

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.272
This is the drill. Ready?

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:30.142
[ Drill whirring ]

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:32.511
-When it's in your brain,
that kind of feeling.

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.546
-Just playing that really hurt
my head.

00:03:34.546 --> 00:03:36.816
It hurt my teeth.
Alright, so that was that.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:40.653
And this is "Bury a Friend," and
you can see if you can hear it.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.489
♪ -Calling security,
keepin' my head held down ♪

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.690
[ Drill whirs ]

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:45.925
♪ Bury the hatchet ♪

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.160
-Wow.
-Just a little textural thing.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.595
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:51.564
-Wow, that's awesome.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:53.866
Now, I have one here,
that says "crosswalk."

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.067
What is "crosswalk"?

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.236
-So, we've toured Australia
a couple times,

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:00.006
which is really beautiful,
and in Sydney and Melbourne,

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.875
when you stand at a street light
and you press the button

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.376
to cross the street, which --

00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:06.278
Here's my poll
that I'm conducting.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:07.780
Do you think those buttons --

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.082
-First of all,
you're not conducting a poll.

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:11.284
-I'm conducting a poll.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:13.486
You're the first person
that I'm polling.

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:16.422
Do you think the buttons --
-I'm sorry, I'm busy right now.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.957
-Yeah, sorry.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.159
-Do you think the buttons on
street corners do anything

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.693
or do you think
they're just for us?

00:04:21.693 --> 00:04:22.895
-They're just for us.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.530
It's almost like the door close
in the elevator.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.365
That doesn't do anything.
-Right, absolutely.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.734
-It's not attached to anything.
Press it as much as you want.

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:30.436
And then eventually
the door will close.

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.405
-The superintendent's like,
"Yeah, I put those --

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.674
I count them every day to see
how many idiots there are."

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:35.808
[ Laughter ]
Anyway.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:37.309
-But I do press the button
all the time.

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:39.212
-Oh, yeah, me too.
You press it over and over.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.146
So, when you're on a street
corner in Australia,

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:44.517
you press the button,
and when the walk sign turns on,

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:46.085
you hear this rhythmic sound

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:48.121
that I love
and that Billie loved.

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:50.623
And so we recorded it,
and it sounds like this.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:54.393
[ Rapid tapping ]

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:57.096
It's got, like, a groove.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.198
♪ -Feels like
the white-winged dove ♪

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:01.267
♪ Sings a song,
sounds like she's singing ♪

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:05.471
♪ Ooh, baby, ooh-ooh ♪

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.540
-Perfect.
-Wait. That's bizarre.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.241
Wait. That happens in Australia?

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:11.944
-That's just every time you
cross a street in Australia,

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:14.080
you hear that.
-Wow.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.449
Alright. So then you use that
for the song "Bad Guy."

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.651
-Yeah, the thing people think
are high hats

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.920
in "Bad Guy"
is actually just that.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.155
♪ -Duh ♪

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:27.460
[ Rapid tapping ]

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.829
-Here's the --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:36.235
-Wow. Wow!

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:40.005
-Here's the bonus round, is that
we then went back to Australia

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:41.174
after the album came out.

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.941
And we brought our dad
for the first time.

00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:45.244
The first couple tours,
we couldn't, like, bring our --

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.046
We just didn't have
enough crew to bring

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:48.614
'cause we have our dad
on our crew,

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:49.915
and we brought our dad with us,

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.550
and we were bringing him
around Australia.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.751
It was his first time.

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:55.488
And he goes, "Check out
these crosswalk sounds."

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:57.457
He goes,
"How cool are these sounds?"

00:05:57.457 --> 00:06:00.459
And I was like, "Yep. Yep."

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:02.261
And I was like,
"Check this out."

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.596
I played him "Bad Guy"
and he was like, "Wha?"

00:06:04.596 --> 00:06:06.499
-And you go --
-Yeah, it was great.

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:08.334
It was a good revelation
for Pops.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.269
-You're like, "Duh."

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.337
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.337 --> 00:06:13.873
Come on.
-That's why he has a show.

